---
import CTASection from '../../components/sections/CTASection.astro'
import CodeBlock from '../../components/ui/CodeBlock.astro'
import HarnessHero from '../../components/harness/HarnessHero.astro'
import HarnessLayout from '../../layouts/HarnessLayout.astro'
---

<HarnessLayout
  title="Guardrails | AI Harness | PURISTA"
  description="Typed, observable content and tool guardrails for PURISTA AI Harness agents and workflows."
>
  <HarnessHero
    lines={[
      { text: 'Safety controls' },
      { text: 'with evidence.', color: 'con' },
    ]}
    lede="Guardrails add typed, fail-closed decisions around a Harness agent loop: input, output, retrieval, tools, and sensitive data. They work alongside Zod, permissions, governance, and application authorization — not instead of them."
  />

  <section class="section-pad bg-[var(--color-bg-sunk)] border-y border-[var(--color-line)]">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head mb-8">
        <div class="eyebrow">Sensitive-data adapters</div>
        <h2 class="display display-lg">Keep detection interchangeable.</h2>
        <p class="lede">The Guardrails addon owns strict policy and enforcement. Your composition root owns the detector and its security boundary.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
        <div class="card"><h3 class="h3">Original Presidio sidecar</h3><p>Install <code>@purista/harness-guardrails-presidio</code> when you need Presidio recognizers such as <code>PERSON</code> or custom deployment-side recognizers. It calls only <code>POST /analyze</code> through an injected authenticated internal HTTP(S) endpoint, never from YAML or a browser.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3 class="h3">Native local subset</h3><p>Install <code>@purista/harness-guardrails-native-privacy</code> for local <code>EMAIL_ADDRESS</code>, <code>PHONE_NUMBER</code>, <code>CREDIT_CARD</code>, <code>IP_ADDRESS</code>, <code>IBAN_CODE</code>, <code>US_SSN</code>, and <code>URL</code> recognition. One Node-API prebuild family is tested with Node.js and Bun on macOS, Linux glibc, and Windows; unsupported platforms fail instead of falling back.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-6 text-[var(--color-fg-muted)]">Masking is provider-neutral and happens in Guardrails after inspection. For structured tool values, provide a reviewed codec that selects exact fields; the addon never recursively scans arbitrary JSON.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-pad">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head mb-8">
        <div class="eyebrow">Deterministic testing</div>
        <h2 class="display display-lg">Script outcomes, never private data.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Use the public fakes for agent, workflow, tool, and adapter tests. They make no recognition or network call: your test supplies each finding, failure, or Presidio HTTP response.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
        <CodeBlock
          language="typescript"
          label="guardrails.test.ts"
          code={`import { FakeSensitiveDataDetector } from '@purista/harness-guardrails/testing'

const detector = new FakeSensitiveDataDetector({
  supportedEntities: ['EMAIL_ADDRESS'],
})
detector.enqueue([
  { category: 'EMAIL_ADDRESS', start: 0, end: 22, score: 0.99 },
])

// The next unscripted inspection is an empty finding list.
// Use enqueueError(...) to exercise the fail-closed path.`}
        />
        <CodeBlock
          language="typescript"
          label="presidio-adapter.test.ts"
          code={`import { createPresidioDetector } from '@purista/harness-guardrails-presidio'
import { FakePresidioSidecar } from '@purista/harness-guardrails-presidio/testing'

const sidecar = new FakePresidioSidecar()
sidecar.enqueueAnalyzeResponse([])
const detector = createPresidioDetector({
  id: 'presidio-test',
  endpoint: 'https://presidio.test/',
  fetch: sidecar.fetch,
})

// Assert sidecar.requests for the POST /analyze wire contract.
// It scripts HTTP/transport behavior; it does not imitate Presidio NLP.`}
        />
      </div>
      <p class="mt-6 text-[var(--color-fg-muted)]">The fakes retain test-only in-memory request data, which may include synthetic input. Never copy it to telemetry, logs, snapshots, or production fixtures.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-pad bg-[var(--color-bg-sunk)] border-y border-[var(--color-line)]">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head--center mb-10">
        <div class="eyebrow">What is protected</div>
        <h2 class="display display-lg">One agent loop, four control points</h2>
        <p class="lede">Attach a portable NeMo-shaped rail configuration to a default-loop agent. Each configured action returns an explicit allow, block, or typed transformation decision.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
        <div class="card">
          <h3 class="h3">Input & output</h3>
          <p>Input rails run after schema parsing and before instructions, transcript, or a model call. Output rails run after a provider result and before validation, persistence, or tool dispatch.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 class="h3">Tools & skills</h3>
          <p>Tool-input rails run before permissions, governance, schema validation, and side effects. Tool-output rails run before the result returns to the model. Skill activation is covered when the agent uses its built-in <code>read</code> tool.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 class="h3">Retrieval</h3>
          <p>Retrieval remains application-owned. Filter JSON-compatible chunks explicitly before adding them to an agent input, so vector-store ownership and retention remain clear.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 class="h3">Evidence without content</h3>
          <p>Every decision is traceable with a rail ID, phase, outcome, duration, and controlled reason code. Prompts, documents, tool values, and secrets stay out of telemetry.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 class="h3">Sensitive data</h3>
          <p>Portable policy can detect or mask configured data categories. Choose an injected Presidio sidecar or the local native subset without coupling policy to a provider or endpoint.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-pad">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head mb-8">
        <div class="eyebrow">Setup</div>
        <h2 class="display display-lg">Keep configuration portable, keep authority local.</h2>
        <p class="lede">The addon reads the compatible YAML vocabulary, but your TypeScript actions own policy implementation. It never creates a provider, reads credentials, or creates a vector store from configuration.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
        <CodeBlock
          language="yaml"
          label="guardrails/config.yaml"
          code={`models:
  - type: main
    engine: harness
    model: assistant

rails:
  config:
    sensitive_data_detection:
      input:
        entities: [EMAIL_ADDRESS]
        mask_token: '<MASKED>'
        score_threshold: 0.6
  input:
    flows: ['mask sensitive data on input', remove-secret-marker]
  output:
    flows: [redact-contact-details]
  tool_input:
    flows: [approve-transfer]`}
        />
        <CodeBlock
          language="typescript"
          label="guardrails.ts"
          code={`import { createSensitiveDataActions, defineGuardrails } from '@purista/harness-guardrails'
import { createPresidioDetector } from '@purista/harness-guardrails-presidio'

const detector = createPresidioDetector({
  id: 'presidio-private',
  endpoint: 'https://presidio.internal/',
})

const rails = defineGuardrails({
  config: await loadGuardrailsConfig('./guardrails'),
  modelAliases: { main: 'assistant' },
  actions: {
    ...createSensitiveDataActions({ detector }),
    'remove-secret-marker': {
      evaluate: ({ value }) => ({
        decision: 'transform',
        target: 'user_message',
        value: redact(value),
        reasonCode: 'secret_redacted',
      }),
    },
    'approve-transfer': {
      evaluate: ({ value }) => isApproved(value)
        ? { decision: 'allow' }
        : { decision: 'block', reasonCode: 'approval_required' },
    },
  },
})`}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-pad bg-[var(--color-bg-sunk)] border-y border-[var(--color-line)]">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head mb-8">
        <div class="eyebrow">Workflow use case</div>
        <h2 class="display display-lg">Guard the agent; orchestrate in the workflow.</h2>
        <p class="lede">A workflow delegates model and tool work to an attached default-loop agent. It filters its own retrieval result before that hand-off. The result is one correlated workflow → guardrail → model/tool trace tree.</p>
      </div>
      <CodeBlock
        language="typescript"
        label="support-workflow.ts"
        code={`const support = rails.attach(agent({
  model: 'assistant',
  instructions: 'Help customers safely.',
  tools: ['transfer_money'],
  skills: ['refund_policy'],
  builtinTools: ['read'],
}))

const answerCustomer = workflow({
  input: z.object({ question: z.string() }),
  output: z.string(),
  delegation: { agents: ['support'] },
  handler: async (ctx) => {
    const chunks = await searchApprovedKnowledge(ctx.input.question)
    const safeChunks = await rails.filterRetrievedChunks(chunks, {
      workflowId: ctx.workflowId,
      runId: ctx.runId,
      sessionId: ctx.sessionId,
      models: ctx.models,
      signal: ctx.signal,
      logger: ctx.log,
    })

    return ctx.agents.support({
      question: ctx.input.question,
      context: safeChunks,
    })
  },
})`}
      />
      <p class="mt-6 text-[var(--color-fg-muted)]">Custom-handler agents and direct <code>ctx.models.*</code> calls own their lifecycle, so they are intentionally not intercepted. The addon rejects <code>rails.attach(...)</code> for a custom handler rather than giving a false sense of coverage.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-pad">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head--center mb-10">
        <div class="eyebrow">Operations</div>
        <h2 class="display display-lg">Trace safety and model cost together.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Guardrail evaluations are OpenTelemetry/OpenInference spans with content-free outcome attributes. A block is a successful control decision; failures and timeouts are errors.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-8">
        <div class="card"><h3 class="h3">Decision trace</h3><p><code>evaluate_guardrail {'{id}'}</code> records rail ID, phase, allow/block/transform/error, duration, and an optional deployment-controlled reason code.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3 class="h3">Metrics</h3><p><code>harness.guardrail.evaluations</code> and <code>harness.guardrail.duration</code> make policy behavior visible without exposing content.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3 class="h3">Safety-model cost</h3><p>A model check nests its normal LLM span below the guardrail span. Provider, model, and reported input/output/total tokens remain canonical there for cost attribution.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card mt-8"><h3 class="h3">Sensitive-data operations</h3><p>Every inspection creates a child <code>harness.sensitive_data.inspect</code> GUARDRAIL span plus inspection and duration metrics. It contains only detector id, local/cloud mode, detect/mask operation, outcome, bounded finding count, configured categories, and failure type. It contains no source text, offsets, endpoint data, model, token, or cost fields.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTASection
    headline={[{ text: 'Build a ' }, { text: 'typed safety boundary', color: 'con' }, { text: '.' }]}
    lede="Use guardrails with narrow schemas, tool allowlists, permissions, governance, and deterministic business authorization."
    ctas={[
      { label: 'Observability', href: '/harness/observability/', variant: 'primary' },
      { label: 'Security', href: '/harness/security/', variant: 'ghost' },
    ]}
  />
</HarnessLayout>
